# Instant UI and Backend Sync

Every UI change on shared backend data must update the acting user immediately and converge across authorized sessions through the standard realtime pipeline.

## End-to-end flow

- Widgets read authoritative data only from Riverpod and delegate actions to controllers; never call APIs or keep competing server-data copies.
- Controllers write through repositories over HTTP; WebSockets are server-to-client sync, never a mutation transport.
- On successful persistence (`saved == true`), immediately patch every affected Riverpod slice from the response or a typed local delta; cancel or failure must not patch or refresh.
- After commit, the backend publishes a scoped domain event for each user-visible shared mutation.
- Controllers subscribe with the relevant `RealtimeEventGroups` and `listenForRealtimeRefresh`.
- Reconcile via `WorkspaceSyncEngine`: apply a safe typed `RealtimeDelta` or the smallest `WorkspaceRefreshPlan` fetch; HTTP resolves conflicts.
- Defer reconciliation while saving; refresh after authenticated reconnect.

## Efficiency

- Prefer the smallest patch; blanket `ref.invalidate` or full reload is a last resort.
- A local mutation must be visible in the same frame or next rebuild, never blocked on a workspace `GET`.
- Must not navigate, show feedback, call `setState`, or mutate widgets from realtime handlers.

## Verification

- Unit-test delta appliers and event-group membership; controller-test success patching, cancel/failure, and save-time deferral.
- Test reconnect refresh and cross-stack backend event emission.

